### Further reading on context as an organisational concern

- Ethan Mollick, [Detecting the Secret Cyborgs](https://www.oneusefulthing.org/p/detecting-the-secret-cyborgs). When orgs focus AI policy on restrictions, employees use AI privately without sharing knowledge. Management's job is creating conditions for context-sharing, not gatekeeping.
- Charity Majors on [observability and relational context](https://charity.wtf/). The three-pillars model destroys relational context. Context preservation is an architectural decision, not an individual engineering task.
- Gene Kim & Steven Spear, [*Wiring the Winning Organization*](https://itrevolution.com/product/wiring-the-winning-organization/). Organisations win through communication and coordination patterns; their three mechanisms (slowification, simplification, amplification) are about making context legible and actionable.
- Shreyas Doshi on [context as organisational literacy](https://shreyasdoshi.com/). Context is a leadership competency, not just domain knowledge.
- Eric Evans, [*Domain-Driven Design*](https://www.domainlanguage.com/ddd/) (2003). The software engineering tradition's version of the same insight: the hard problem is shared understanding of the domain, not the implementation. Ubiquitous language and bounded contexts are context infrastructure formalised as engineering practice.

